+
+### Current hand-item anchors during editor acquisition — 2026-09-15
+
+`PlayerHumanoidRig.resolve_authoring_hand_item_anchor_world_transform_state(slot)`
+reads the current bone pose during `editor_preview`. It does not read the possibly
+delayed world frame of `BoneAttachment3D`, advance a solver, or write a pose.
+Its per-call registry declares the full chain:
+
+```text
+RightHandItemAnchorOrigin / LeftHandItemAnchorOrigin
+  -> RightWristOrigin / LeftWristOrigin
+  -> RL_BoneRoot
+  -> machine_to_world presentation
+```
+
+The anchor-to-wrist edge is the existing authored item-anchor `Transform3D`.
+The wrist-to-machine edge is `inverse(root pose) * current Hand pose`; both records
+are owned by `player_humanoid_rig` in `editor_preview`. Basis and model scale are
+retained. Invalid ancestry, attachment, or transforms return `valid=false` without
+a usable frame. The public settled wrist capture used by Roll remains a separate
+`post_final_pose` read.
+
+During authoring, mounting and the local alignment-offset conversion use this same
+current anchor frame. The existing `hand_alignment_offset_local` field retains its
+`HandGripAlignmentOrigin` ID. Its returned state now provides a registry where that
+origin is an identity child of the selected item-anchor origin, matching the frame
+in which the offset is measured. Converting the offset through that chain yields
+the existing anatomical contact point; this does not move its definition to the
+wrist. The reader changes are confined to editor authoring. Runtime offset reads
+and the later F/save integration have not been migrated by this change.
